{
  "summary": "This computer has two remote-control doors open to the network — TightVNC and Remote Desktop (without its extra login check) — and the Windows Firewall is switched off for the Public profile, the setting used on networks like cafés or hotels. Together that means other computers on such a network could reach these login screens directly. First, turn the Public firewall back on, then decide whether you actually use TightVNC and Remote Desktop. Also take a quick look at the extra administrator account to confirm you recognize it.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Firewall is off on public networks",
      "finding_ids": [
        "FW-PROFILE-OFF:d0633f5d"
      ],
      "explanation": "The firewall is the barrier that blocks unwanted connections from other devices. It is off for the Public profile, which Windows uses on networks you don't trust, such as café, airport or hotel Wi‑Fi. With it off, the remote-login services below are reachable by anyone on that network. Turning it back on is the single most protective step; the report shows how for this finding."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "TightVNC remote-control software is running and accepting connections",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:7cb92661",
        "REMOTE-ACCESS-TOOL:cb90e00a"
      ],
      "explanation": "TightVNC is installed (C:\\Program Files\\TightVNC\\tvnserver.exe) and is listening on port 5900 on all network connections, so other computers can try to connect and control this one. If you or someone helping you set it up on purpose, it can be fine, but it should be protected and ideally only reachable where needed. If you don't recognize it, treat it seriously, because tools like this can be how someone gets in. See the fixes listed for these findings."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Remote Desktop is on without Network Level Authentication",
      "finding_ids": [
        "RDP-NLA-OFF:8e943d20",
        "RDP-ENABLED:62fe1d90",
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:b128d5fc"
      ],
      "explanation": "These three findings describe one thing: Windows Remote Desktop is turned on, listening on port 3389 on all network connections, and it doesn't require Network Level Authentication (an extra check that makes people prove who they are before they even see the login screen). Remote Desktop is one of the most attacked services on Windows, so if you don't use it, turning it off removes the risk; if you do, requiring NLA is an important improvement. The report shows the fix for each."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "An additional administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is an administrator account named <computer>\\<account-2> on this computer. Having a separate admin account is often good practice and this is usually normal. Just confirm it's one you or someone you trust created. Because remote-access services are exposed here, it's worth a quick check."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
